            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "CPeck"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I just made this and it's excellent!! I used how ever many tomatillos I had from my garden (they're smaller than you'd usually get from the grocery store) and it tastes awesome! Especially love this because I didn't have to skin the tomatillos and peppers (a big pain in the butt for some of the smaller ones that I had). I also added the juice from one lime; I like the subtle citrus that it adds to guac and salsas."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "2"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "gwammy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This recipe is most improved by roasting the tomatillos and jalapenos in the oven for about 20 mins. at 400 degree, until they're a little toasted. You can easily peel off any burned parts by quickly placing them in a plastic bag to steam the skins a little. This method brings out more flavor than boiling.",
            "datePublished": "2017-12-12T05:59:08.577Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "FishyBeta"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I really enjoyed this recipe. To kick it up a level, I added roasted poblano peppers instead of jalapenos, and a touch of sour cream. The sour cream is a great way to balance the spice; you get a kick of spice, but it fades away without burning your mouth. I served this with chips, breakfast tacos, enchiladas, roasted potatoes, as a salad dressing, and so on. It even freezes well, so you can make extra for later.",
            "datePublished": "2021-02-16T21:32:49.133Z"
          },
